  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A five-story Antarctic waterfall bleeds bright crimson red. Discovered on Taylor Glacier in 1911, it looked like fresh blood pouring over pure white ice.",
        "visual_keywords": "glacier ice frozen landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scientists originally blamed red algae. But tests found zero plant life\u2014and revealed the water stayed liquid far below freezing temperatures.",
        "visual_keywords": "microscope research lab laboratory"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How could flowing water exist in a frozen wasteland? Researchers scanned four hundred meters down with deep radar and uncovered the hidden source.",
        "visual_keywords": "radar screen technology ice"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Trapped below was an ancient, super-salty lake sealed without light for two million years. Its massive iron content rusts instantly when hitting air.",
        "visual_keywords": "underwater dark lake rust"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That red waterfall is actually liquid rust. Even wilder, ancient microbes thrive inside it without oxygen\u2014proving life can survive on alien worlds.",
        "visual_keywords": "space stars alien planet"
      }
    ],
